## 132. Nested Shapes

Example pattern:

```text
Circle inside square
Circle inside triangle
Circle inside pentagon
Triangle inside circle
```

The last may differ because the inner/outer relationship is reversed.

---

## 133. Symmetry Method

If:

```text
Figure A → 1 axis
Figure B → 1 axis
Figure C → 1 axis
Figure D → 2 axes
```

D may be the outlier.

---

## 134. Classification Using Graphs

For complex figures, treat each object as a graph:

```text
Nodes = shapes/elements
Edges = connections
```

Example:

```text
A: 3 nodes, 2 connections
B: 3 nodes, 2 connections
C: 3 nodes, 2 connections
D: 4 nodes, 3 connections
```

D differs structurally.

---

## 135. Classification and Invariants

An **invariant** is a property that remains unchanged under a transformation.

For example, rotation preserves:

- number of sides
- number of dots
- connectivity

So if three figures are rotations of each other, they share these invariants.

The odd figure violates an invariant.

---

## 136. Invariant Diagram

```text
Figure A ─rotation─> Figure B
      │
      └── same number of elements

Figure C ─rotation─> Figure D

Odd figure:
different invariant
```

---

## 137. Advanced Classification Strategy

For difficult questions, create a property table.

Example:

| Item | Even | Prime | Square | 3× | 5× |
|---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|
| 4 | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |
| 9 | ✗ | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ |
| 16 | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |
| 25 | ✗ | ✗ | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ |
| 27 | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✓ | ✗ |

The dominant shared property is "perfect square" for the first four.

27 is the outlier.

---

## 138. Outlier Score

Conceptually, define:

```text
O(x)=1-\frac{\text{number of shared properties}}{\text{properties tested}}
```

The item with the highest meaningful outlier score is a candidate.

This is a reasoning model, not an exam formula.

---

## 139. Cluster Model

Suppose:

```text
A = animal + mammal
B = animal + mammal
C = animal + mammal
D = plant + living
```

Three form one cluster.

```text
       A
      / \
     B---C

          D
```

D is outside the cluster.

---

## 140. Hierarchical Classification

Categories can be nested:

```text
Living
├── Plants
│   ├── Trees
│   └── Flowers
└── Animals
    ├── Mammals
    └── Birds
```

If three items belong to one branch and one to another, the branch difference may be the answer.

---

## 141. Category-Level Matching

Important rule:

> Compare items at the same level.

Example:

```text
India : Country
Delhi : City
```

These are different category levels.

For classification, a better comparison is:

```text
India
China
Japan
France
```

All are countries.

---

## 142. Classification by Relation

Sometimes the items themselves are pairs:

```text
Bird : Nest
Dog : Kennel
Lion : Den
Fish : Water
```

The first three are:

```text
Animal → Shelter/Home
```

Fish → Water is:

```text
Animal → Environment
```

Depending on the question, Fish may be the odd pair.

---

## 143. Pair Classification

For paired items, do not classify each word separately.

Classify the **relationship**.

```text
A : B
C : D
E : F
G : H
```

Find the pair whose internal relation differs.

---

## 144. Pair-Relation Vector

Represent:

```text
P=(X,Y,R)
```

where \(R\) is the relationship.

Example:

```text
Bird : Nest
R = lives in
```

```text
Pen : Write
R = used for
```

If three pairs have the same relation type and one does not, the different pair is the outlier.

---

## 145. Common Pair Relationships

```text
Person → Workplace
Animal → Home
Tool → Function
Part → Whole
Cause → Effect
Country → Capital
Object → Material
Word → Synonym
Number → Square
Letter → Shift
```

---

## 146. Classification vs Analogy

| Classification | Analogy |
|---|---|
| Find the different item | Find missing relationship |
| A, B, C, D | A:B :: C:? |
| Grouping problem | Transformation/relation problem |
| Focus on shared property | Focus on same relationship |

---
